## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests advanced dynamic programming: partitioning with a non-trivial segment cost that depends on min and max, requiring careful state design and in-loop maintenance of subproblem results. It also validates producing both memoized and iterative solutions and analyzing time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- DP with partition states
- In-loop maintenance of running min and max
- Top-down memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Time complexity: O(D*N^2); Space: O(D*N)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Top-down memoized
function minCost(A, D){
  const N = A.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(null));
  function solve(d, i){
    if (d === 1){
      let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity;
      for (let k = 0; k <= i; k++){ mn = Math.min(mn, A[k]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[k]); }
      return mn * mx;
    }
    if (memo[d][i] != null) return memo[d][i];
    let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity; let best = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      best = Math.min(best, solve(d-1, j-1) + mn*mx);
    }
    memo[d][i] = best; return best;
  }
  return solve(D, N-1);
}

// Bottom-up
function minCostBottom(A, D){
  const N = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(INF));
  // base: d = 1
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++){
    let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      if (j === 0) dp[1][i] = Math.min(dp[1][i], mn*mx);
    }
  }
  for (let d = 2; d <= D; d++){
    for (let i = d-1; i < N; i++){
      let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
      for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
        mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
        dp[d][i] = Math.min(dp[d][i], dp[d-1][j-1] + mn*mx);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[D][N-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

The problem mirrors real-world build systems and DAG validations, requiring both cycle detection and a valid execution order.

## Key Concepts

- DFS with 3-color marking to detect cycles
- Topological sort via postorder push
- Handling multiple components in a possibly disconnected graph

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfsTopo(graph) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const color = Array(n).fill(0); // 0 white, 1 gray, 2 black
  const order = [];
  const cycles = [];
  function dfs(u) {
    color[u] = 1;
    for (const v of graph[u] || []) {
      if (color[v] === 0) {
        if (dfs(v)) return true;
      } else if (color[v] === 1) {
        cycles.push([u, v]);
        return true;
      }
    }
    color[u] = 2;
    order.push(u);
    return false;
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (color[i] === 0) {
      if (dfs(i)) break;
    }
  }
  return { topo: order.reverse(), cycle: cycles.length ? cycles : null };
}
```

