- How would you return the exact cycle path rather than just edge pairs?
- What are the time and space complexities for dense vs sparse graphs?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates blending two classic sorts to optimize real workloads and caching behavior, plus reasoning about stability and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Hybrid sorting (Introsort-like) with depth-limited quicksort
- Threshold tuning for switching to mergesort
- Stability in final output and how to preserve it
- Complexity: worst-case O(n log n) via depth cap, average-case O(n log n); extra space for merges

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, lo=0, hi=arr.length-1, depth=0) {
  const n = hi - lo + 1;
  if (n <= 32) return stableMergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
  if (depth > 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length))) return heapSort(arr, lo, hi);
  const p = partition(arr, lo, hi);
  hybridSort(arr, lo, p-1, depth+1);
  hybridSort(arr, p+1, hi, depth+1);
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you benchmark the threshold and pivot strategy across diverse datasets?
- How would you adapt for strict in-place constraints and full stability?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses interval DP, proper subproblem decomposition, and practical optimization with prefix sums. Builds intuition for product costs over contiguous blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on [i, j]
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Bottom-up filling by increasing length; base case 0 for single element

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMergeCost(stones){
  const n = stones.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + stones[i];
  const sum = (i,j) => pref[j+1]-pref[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j = i+len-1;
      let best = Infinity;
      for(let k=i; k<j; k++){
        const cost = dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + sum(i,j);
        if(cost < best) best = cost;
      }
      dp[i][j] = best;
    }
  }
  return n>0 ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for K-ary merging (K > 2)?
- What optimizations apply if n is large and many merges share sums?

